\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Rows record target packets and columns record source packets. This convention fixes execution order. Composable words collapse to endpoint blocks, while one broken packet interface annihilates the entire operator product.}}{3}{figure.caption.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Exact coordinate transport and connection transport are distinct. The canonical loop telescopes to the identity. A chosen connection may carry nontrivial holonomy, which transforms by conjugation under gauge.}}{11}{figure.caption.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces A finite complete packet resolution reconstructs the total holonomy obstruction from blocks indexed by input and output packets. A nonzero off-diagonal block is an exact witness of packet mixing.}}{18}{figure.caption.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Second packet jets for $F(x,y)=(x+xy,y+x^2)$ at the origin. Coordinate axis projectors give $DF(0)=I$. The first output has mixed second derivatives equal to one, while the second has a repeated first input derivative equal to two. Each grid records the ordered pair of input packets. The mixed entries retain an interaction that diagonal restrictions would lose.}}{34}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces A specified correction range in $\symbb R^2$. For $L(a,b)=(a,0)$, the image is the horizontal axis. The defect $(2,3)$ splits into $(2,0)$ in that image and the remaining representative $(0,3)$. Subtracting the image component leaves the same cokernel class. The functional $(x,y)\mapsto y$ vanishes on the correction range and detects the value three. The correction equation $Lu=-(2,3)$ therefore has no solution.}}{45}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces A rotating rank one packet and its orthogonal complement. With $u(\mittheta )=(\cos \mittheta ,\sin \mittheta )$ and $P(\mittheta )=u(\mittheta )u(\mittheta )^{\mathsf T}$, the canonical generator is $[P',P]=\bigl (\begin {smallmatrix}0&-1 1&0\end {smallmatrix}\bigr )$. For a positive step $\mitdelta <\mitpi /2$, the polar unitary is the usual rotation $R(\mitdelta )$. On each displayed mesh, the direct rotation product equals $R(\mitpi /3)$ exactly. This special planar example illustrates the construction. The general theorem supplies convergence for families whose step products need not already equal the continuous transport.}}{66}{figure.caption.6}%
